*  PERM_driver - exercises PERM(A): Trotter's algorithm cycling through
*  every permutation of a singly-dimensioned array with lower bound 1,
*  failing when none remain; A is assumed to have at least 2 elements.
*  Contract per PERM.sno header (whose 1999 note records that an erroneous
*  EQ(D,1) term once produced DUPLICATE permutations -- so the COUNT is part
*  of what is being checked here).  Cases: three elements enumerated with a
*  count, then two elements.  Note what the oracle .ref records and do not
*  read it as a repeat defect: the FIRST call is an initialisation that does
*  not advance A, so under this print-then-advance loop the starting
*  arrangement is printed twice and the tally runs one above 2! and 3!.
-INCLUDE "PERM.sno"
        A  =  ARRAY(3)
        A<1>  =  '1'
        A<2>  =  '2'
        A<3>  =  '3'
        N  =  0
P3      N  =  N + 1
        OUTPUT  =  A<1> A<2> A<3>
        PERM(A)						:S(P3)
        OUTPUT  =  'three elements gave ' N ' permutations'
        B  =  ARRAY(2)
        B<1>  =  'x'
        B<2>  =  'y'
        M  =  0
P2      M  =  M + 1
        OUTPUT  =  B<1> B<2>
        PERM(B)						:S(P2)
        OUTPUT  =  'two elements gave ' M ' permutations'
END
